/**

  @page install_vcpkg Building %OpenMS with vcpkg

  This document describes how to build %OpenMS using vcpkg for dependency management.
  This is the recommended approach for building %OpenMS from source as it provides
  a consistent, cross-platform way to manage dependencies.

  @section install_vcpkg_overview Overview

  vcpkg is a C++ package manager that works on Windows, macOS, and Linux. %OpenMS
  includes a `vcpkg.json` manifest file that lists all required dependencies. When
  building %OpenMS with vcpkg, all dependencies will be automatically installed
  in a consistent way across all platforms.

  @section install_vcpkg_prerequisites Prerequisites

  Before building %OpenMS with vcpkg, you'll need:
  - Git
  - CMake (>= 3.21)
  - A C++ compiler (GCC >= 11, Clang >= 15, or MSVC >= 14.36)
  - Ninja (recommended build system)

  @section install_vcpkg_setup Setting up vcpkg

  1. Clone the vcpkg repository:
  
  \code{.sh}
  git clone https://github.com/microsoft/vcpkg.git
  cd vcpkg
  \endcode

  2. Bootstrap vcpkg:

  On Linux/macOS:
  \code{.sh}
  ./bootstrap-vcpkg.sh
  \endcode

  On Windows:
  \code{.bat}
  .\bootstrap-vcpkg.bat
  \endcode

  3. Set the VCPKG_ROOT environment variable (optional but recommended):

  On Linux/macOS:
  \code{.sh}
  export VCPKG_ROOT=/path/to/vcpkg
  \endcode

  On Windows (PowerShell):
  \code{.ps1}
  $env:VCPKG_ROOT = "C:\path\to\vcpkg"
  \endcode

  @section install_vcpkg_build Building OpenMS

  1. Clone the %OpenMS repository:

  \code{.sh}
  git clone https://github.com/OpenMS/OpenMS.git
  cd OpenMS
  git submodule update --init THIRDPARTY
  \endcode

  2. Configure the build with vcpkg toolchain:

  \code{.sh}
  mkdir build
  cd build
  cmake .. \
    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=/path/to/vcpkg/scripts/buildsystems/vcpkg.cmake \
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
    -G Ninja
  \endcode

  On Windows with Visual Studio:
  \code{.bat}
  cmake .. ^
    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=C:\path\to\vcpkg\scripts\buildsystems\vcpkg.cmake ^
    -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release ^
    -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" ^
    -A x64
  \endcode

  3. Build %OpenMS:

  \code{.sh}
  cmake --build . --config Release
  \endcode

  @section install_vcpkg_dependencies Dependencies

  The `vcpkg.json` file in the %OpenMS root directory declares all required
  dependencies. vcpkg will automatically install these when you configure
  the build. The main dependencies include:

  - Boost (multiple components)
  - Qt6 (qtbase, qtsvg)
  - Eigen3
  - Xerces-C
  - COIN-OR (CBC, CGL, CLP)
  - libsvm
  - Apache Arrow (for Parquet support)
  - zlib, bzip2

  @section install_vcpkg_custom Custom Qt Packages

  %OpenMS includes custom vcpkg overlay ports for Qt packages in the
  `vcpkg-overlays/ports` directory. These are automatically used when
  building with the vcpkg toolchain. The overlays ensure specific Qt
  versions are used that are compatible with %OpenMS.

  @section install_vcpkg_cache Caching Dependencies

  To speed up subsequent builds, you can cache the vcpkg installed packages:

  - The installed packages are located in `{vcpkg-root}/installed`
  - Build artifacts are in `{vcpkg-root}/buildtrees` and `{vcpkg-root}/packages`

  In CI environments, these directories can be cached to avoid reinstalling
  dependencies on every build.

  @section install_vcpkg_advantages Advantages of vcpkg

  - **Cross-platform**: Same build process on Windows, macOS, and Linux
  - **Consistent versions**: All developers and CI systems use the same library versions
  - **Reproducible builds**: The `vcpkg.json` manifest ensures consistent dependencies
  - **No manual dependency hunting**: vcpkg handles all dependency resolution
  - **Automatic updates**: Updating dependencies is as simple as updating vcpkg

  @section install_vcpkg_troubleshooting Troubleshooting

  @subsection install_vcpkg_troubleshooting_clean Clean Build

  If you encounter issues, try cleaning the vcpkg cache:

  \code{.sh}
  /path/to/vcpkg/vcpkg remove --outdated --recurse
  \endcode

  @subsection install_vcpkg_troubleshooting_overlay Overlay Ports

  If you encounter issues with Qt packages, ensure the overlay ports are being used:

  \code{.sh}
  export VCPKG_OVERLAY_PORTS=/path/to/OpenMS/vcpkg-overlays/ports
  \endcode

  This is automatically set when using the CMake toolchain file, but may need
  to be set manually in some cases.

  @subsection install_vcpkg_troubleshooting_logs Build Logs

  vcpkg provides detailed build logs in `{vcpkg-root}/buildtrees/{package}/`.
  Check these logs if a package fails to build.

  [issues]: https://github.com/OpenMS/OpenMS/issues

*/
